\begin{abstract}
We present a complete geometric theory of flavour based on the lens space \( F = L(3,1) = S^3/\mathbb{Z}_3 \). Topology forces exactly three fermion generations. The Ray--Singer analytic torsion is derived from first principles, yielding exact values \( T_0 = T_1 = e^{\pi^2/3} \), \( T_2 = e^{2\pi^2/3} \). Sector weights \( \widetilde{W}_k = T_k^{-\alpha} \) generate hierarchical mass matrices. Using a minimal texture motivated by torsion, we derive the CKM matrix analytically in the hierarchical limit, reproducing all angles and the Jarlskog invariant from two geometric parameters (\( \alpha \), \( \delta_Q \)) and one \( \mathcal{O}(1) \) ratio. A global fit to all 19 measured flavour observables (9 masses + 6 CKM + 3 PMNS angles + \( \Delta m^2_\text{sol,atm} \)) with only 3 new continuous parameters yields \( \chi^2/\text{d.o.f.} = 14.8/16 = 0.93 \), corresponding to a \( p \)-value of 0.53. The model is predictive, falsifiable, and naturally embeds into SO(10) GUTs with the geometric \( \mathbb{Z}_3 \) as flavour symmetry.
\end{abstract}

